Use the environment's C compiler and C17 for generated code

Two problems when using this driver as installed on Gentoo:

1. CC and LINKER were hardcoded to gcc, so the toolchain selected by the
   ebuild (and any non-gcc default system compiler) was ignored.

2. Copper emits identifiers named "true" and "false" (see e.g. the
   identifiers struct in the generated output).  Those are keywords in C23,
   which GCC 15 defaults to, so compiling any generated C file failed with
   "expected identifier or '(' before 'true'".  Request C17 explicitly,
   matching what the out-of-tree boot compile uses.

--- a/scripts/copper
+++ b/scripts/copper
@@ -59,8 +59,8 @@
 COPPER=
 BACKEND=elf64
 LLC=llc
-CC=gcc
-LINKER=gcc
+CC=${CC:-cc}
+LINKER=${LINKER:-cc}
 INPUT=
 OUT=main
 LINK=1
@@ -221,7 +221,9 @@
 elif  [[ ${BACKEND} == "c" ]]; then
 
 	run ${COPPER} -o ${OUT_C} ${COPPER_FLAGS} ${INPUT}
-	run ${CC} -c -fshort-wchar ${OUT_C} -o ${OUT_OBJ}
+	# Copper emits identifiers called 'true'/'false', which are keywords in
+	# C23 (the default since GCC 15), so ask for C17 explicitly.
+	run ${CC} -std=gnu17 -c -fshort-wchar ${OUT_C} -o ${OUT_OBJ}
 	rm ${OUT_C}
 
 fi
